{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"gradient gradient","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient.md","fileName":"Gradient.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Gradient","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 00:13\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Optimization and Differentiation]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Optimization and Differentiation","Vector","Partial Derivative","Neural Network Parameter Update","Directional Derivative","Stationary Point","algorithms.epub","appliedlinearalgebraandoptimizationusingmatlab.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"gradient gradient","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient.md","fileName":"Gradient.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Gradient","heading":"Gradient","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A gradient is a [[Vector]] containing all the [[Partial Derivative|partial derivatives]] of a multivariable function. It indicates the local direction in which the function increases most directly.\n\nTo reduce neural-network loss, a [[Neural Network Parameter Update]] changes each weight and bias in the direction opposite its corresponding gradient component. This generalizes using the opposite of a single slope to move downhill.\n\nFor any unit direction $u$, the [[Directional Derivative]] is $\\nabla f\\cdot u$. The gradient therefore points in the direction of steepest local increase, its negative points toward steepest decrease, and a zero gradient identifies a [[Stationary Point]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Optimization and Differentiation","Vector","Partial Derivative","Neural Network Parameter Update","Directional Derivative","Stationary P